ir3: round integer-to-float conversions to nearest when nothing says otherwise.

The a6xx `cov` instruction has a rounding-mode field, and create_cov() sets it
only from float_controls_execution_mode, which is a SPIR-V declaration. When
nothing is declared, nir_get_rounding_mode_from_float_controls returns
nir_rounding_mode_undef, neither branch fires, and the field keeps the zero it
was built with -- ROUND_ZERO in instr-a3xx.h, which is truncation toward zero.

So every shader that does not come from SPIR-V with an explicit rounding mode
gets truncation where IEEE-754, GLSL and OpenCL all require round-to-nearest-even.
On this port that is every shader: the port's OpenGL ES declares nothing, and so
does the AIR frontend. The hardware is not at fault -- it can round, and setting
the field makes it.

Measured on the phone's Adreno 7c+ Gen 3, uint -> float, GLSL ES 3.0 through
OSMesa with the value in a uniform so nothing could be folded on the host
(userland/src/u2f_test.c, RC=rc.u2f), expectations from C on the host:

      input         before        after      C
  0xFFFFFFFF    4294967040   4294967296   4294967296
  0xFFFFFF80    4294967040   4294967296   4294967296
  0x01000001      16777216     16777216     16777216
  0x01000003      16777218     16777220     16777220
  0x7FFFFFFF    2147483520   2147483648   2147483648
  0x00FFFFFF      16777215     16777215     16777215

0x01000003 is the value that identifies the rule rather than merely showing a
disagreement: it is 2^24+3, half way between the representable 2^24+2 and
2^24+4, and both round-half-to-even and round-half-away-from-zero give 2^24+4.
The hardware gave 2^24+2, which is neither -- it is the low bits dropped. The
two inputs that agree before the change are exactly the two where truncation and
rounding coincide, so agreement there is the absence of a question.

Reproduced independently down a second path with no GLSL in it at all: the AIR
frontend's own test suite (tools/air/intrincheck.py, docs/air.md section 4)
compiles one Metal operation per module and executes it on the same GPU, and
air.convert.f.{f32,v2f32,v3f32}.{u,s}.* disagree with C on exactly the inputs
that need rounding -- 5 declarations, and i2f truncates just as u2f does. Two
unrelated front ends reaching the same instruction return the same wrong number.

The case for this change does not need a standard quoted at it, because Mesa
already contains both answers. NIR's own constant folder, with no rounding mode
declared, rounds to nearest even: nir_opcodes.py generates, for the plain f2f16,

    if (nir_is_rounding_mode_rtz(execution_mode, 16))
       dst = _mesa_half_to_float(_mesa_float_to_float16_rtz(src0));
    else
       dst = _mesa_half_to_float(_mesa_float_to_float16_rtne(src0));

-- RTZ only where RTZ was asked for, RTNE otherwise -- and it folds an integer
to float conversion through the host's own, which is also RTNE. The backend, in
the same circumstances, leaves the cov field at ROUND_ZERO and truncates.

So one expression has two answers in one compiler depending on whether NIR could
fold it. That is what the measurements above are: the same operation over the
same inputs, folded by NIR and executed by the hardware, disagreeing. Whatever
any specification requires, these two should not differ from each other, and
making the backend ask for what the folder already assumes is the smaller change
of the two.

Not verified: whether the field is honoured for a 16-bit destination. The branch
covers TYPE_F16 too, and the measurements do not reach it -- this port's harness
packs every half result into a float4, so no shader it generates declares a half
output, and a half render target appears in none of the 456 modules of the corpus
it was built from.

Worth a reviewer's attention rather than hidden: one F16 path through this branch
is pack_half_2x16, and nir_lower_mediump.c says of it "pack_half rounding is
undefined". Where nothing is required, setting ROUND_EVEN changes behaviour
without correcting it -- permitted either way, and not part of the argument here.
If that is unwelcome, the narrower change is to set the default only when the
source is an integer type; the measurements above would be unaffected. I would
not choose it, and the reason is worth stating rather than leaving the two
looking equivalent.

Of everything that reaches this branch, pack_half is the only destination with no
requirement behind it. f2f32 and b2f are exact, so the mode cannot change them.
i2f and u2f require round-to-nearest-even, and are measured above. And plain
nir_op_f2f16 reaches it too -- f2f16_rtne and f2f16_rtz are taken by the two
cases above, so an f32 -> f16 narrowing with no declared mode lands here and
truncates by exactly the same mechanism, in an API that asks for RTE. The
narrower change would leave that one unfixed while fixing its neighbours, which
is a worse place to stop than either end.

That last one is reasoning from the mechanism and is NOT measured here: this
port's harness packs every half result into a float4, so it cannot reach an
f2f16 either. It is offered as a thing to check, not as a result.

Checked against Mesa 25.0.7 (this port's pin), the 26.2.5 release, and main as
fetched on 20 October 2026: the rounding block of create_cov() is 750 characters
in each and character-for-character identical in all three, with no trailing
else. So this is not an artefact of the pin, and not something a newer release
has already addressed. (ROUND_ZERO = 0 in instr-a3xx.h was verified in the pin
and in 26.2.5; only ir3_compiler_nir.c was taken from main, so that header is
not claimed for it.)

The hunk applies to main's own copy of the file without a rebase, tested against
it rather than against the pin: both patch(1) and git apply report "succeeded at
410 (offset 2 lines)" and exit 0. The context matches; only the line number has
moved, so anyone comparing the hunk header's 408 against main will see the two
lines and should not take them for a mismatch.

--- a/src/freedreno/ir3/ir3_compiler_nir.c
+++ b/src/freedreno/ir3/ir3_compiler_nir.c
@@ -408,6 +408,13 @@
          set_cat1_round(cov.rpts, nrpt, ROUND_EVEN);
       else if (rounding_mode == nir_rounding_mode_rtz)
          set_cat1_round(cov.rpts, nrpt, ROUND_ZERO);
+      else
+         /* Nothing declared a mode, and the field defaults to ROUND_ZERO,
+          * which is truncation. IEEE-754, GLSL and OpenCL all require
+          * round-to-nearest-even here, so ask for it rather than letting the
+          * hardware's own default stand.
+          */
+         set_cat1_round(cov.rpts, nrpt, ROUND_EVEN);
    }
 
    return cov;
